 ## Changelog
 
+- 2026-10-21 (5): leaf culling goes to depth 1 while the leaf tiles are opaque (Xenon 20.1 `LeafCulling`: SOLID / SOLID_AGGRESSIVE end state; idea only, no code copied) (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run; tests `mesh::tests::{the_shipped_leaf_tiles_are_opaque, leaves_hide_their_faces_against_each_other, a_row_of_leaves_is_a_box}` replace `leaves_keep_the_faces_between_them` and `deep_leaf_faces_are_culled`; run `cargo test --lib --release mesh` first; the expected counts (26 / 13 / 54 / 10) were checked with a Python model of the rule). Answers the open question of entry (3): the leaf tiles the mesher uses (53, 133, 255, `atlas::terrain_tile`) have 0 texels with alpha < 128 in `terrain.rgba` (read with Python; the fancy tiles 52 and 132 have 101 and 90), so a leaf is an opaque cube and a face between two leaves can never be seen. `render/mesh.rs`: `LEAF_DEPTH` (const 2) is now `leaf_depth()`: 1 while no leaf tile is cut-out (every leaf-leaf face dropped: a 3 x 3 x 3 crown is its 54 outside quads, was 162), 2 (the HOLLOW rule of entry (3), unchanged) as soon as an atlas has a leaf tile with a see-through texel, so a fancy atlas still works. `render/atlas.rs`: `tile_is_cutout(tile)`. Xenon's separate "solid layer for enclosed leaves" is a no-op here: `build_job` already splits by tile alpha, so opaque leaf tiles are in the `solid` layer (no `discard`).
+    - Not done: a Fancy / Fast option (`fancy_graphics` still does nothing; with fancy leaves `leaf_depth()` would have to read it). Unmeasured on a device: compare the quad count and the `FPS:` line in a forest.
 - 2026-10-21 (4): upload budget (Sodium `UploadResourceBudget` / `LimitedResourceBudget` / `UnlimitedResourceBudget` / `UploadDurationEstimator` / `ExpDecayLinear2DEstimator`; read from `sodium-dev`: the estimation package and `RenderSectionManager`) (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run; +10 tests in `embeddium::upload_budget`, run `cargo test -p embeddium` first). `embeddium/src/upload_budget.rs`: `UploadBudget` (limited / unlimited, `is_available`, `fits`, `consume`), `UploadDurationEstimator` (linear fit of upload ns over bytes, batches of 100, the first 100 samples weigh equally, then a batch weighs 5%, negative slope clamped), the frame constants (30% of the average frame, floor 2 ms, average frame EMA 5% clamped 1..100 ms). `world/chunks.rs`: `mesh_pending` builds a `UploadBudget` per frame (`frame_duration_budget(average frame)`, `UPLOAD_SIZE_LIMIT` 1 MiB) and uploads finished meshes while they fit: the first one always, a later one only if its bytes and its estimated cost fit; one that does not is kept in `ChunkManager::held` for the next frame (its chunk stays in `meshing`, so no second job). Each `Pages::put` is timed; a frame with writes gives the estimator one sample (average size, average ns), like Sodium's per-frame sample; a `put` that created a page is not sampled (buffer creation is not upload cost). The average frame length is measured between `update` calls. `MESH_PER_FRAME` 2 -> 8 (guard only), `CHUNK_WORK_BUDGET` removed (the 6 ms test on the chunk work of the frame is gone: stream / populate / light no longer shorten the upload).
     - Differences from Sodium, on purpose: (1) Sodium budgets when it SUBMITS a job, from an estimated size (`MeshTaskSizeEstimator`, `JobDurationEstimator`); here the job already ran on a worker and its size is exact, so only the upload is budgeted and the two size / job estimators are not ported. (2) Sodium's `MIN_UPLOAD_DURATION_BUDGET` is written `10_000_000L` with the comment "2ms"; 2 ms is used (10 ms would be a whole 60 Hz frame). (3) Sodium's size limit is the staging buffer's capacity (`getUploadSizeLimit`); wgpu's `write_buffer` has none to ask, so it is the constant 1 MiB. (4) with every size equal Sodium's fit returns before setting the line (it predicts 0 ns); here the line is flat through the mean. (5) `consume` takes the measured time of the upload, not an estimate (Sodium consumes the estimate when it submits).
     - UNVERIFIED: that it compiles; that `write_buffer`'s CPU time says anything about the GPU copy (on Vulkan it is a memcpy into wgpu's staging belt, the copy itself runs at submit, so the estimator learns the CPU side only: if the hitches are on the GPU side the budget will not see them); `UPLOAD_SIZE_LIMIT` and the 30% share are Sodium's / a guess, not tuned. Compare the `FPS:` / `CPU ms/frame` line and the `slow chunk work` warnings while walking into fresh terrain, and how fast the ring fills (the lever if it is too slow: `UPLOAD_SIZE_LIMIT`).
